---
layout: article
title: Update a rule
description: Change an existing Appwrite Firewall rule's name, scope, conditions, action settings, priority, or enabled state.
---

You can update Firewall rules from the rules list without recreating them. Changes apply to new requests after save. Historical metrics are not rewritten.

# Open update {% #open-update %}

1. Open **Firewall** in your project.
2. Select the **API**, **Functions**, or **Sites** tab that contains the rule.
3. Open the rule's actions menu and choose **Update**, or use the update action from the rule context menu.

# What you can change {% #what-you-can-change %}

| Field | Notes |
|-------|-------|
| Name and description | Labels only; no traffic impact |
| Resource type and resource ID | Move the rule between API, Functions, and Sites (pick a function or site when required) |
| Conditions | Rebuilt with the same condition builder as create; at least one complete condition required |
| Rate limit settings | Request limit, interval, and limit by when the rule action is rate limit. Token-bucket rules can also change **Max bucket size**. |
| Redirect settings | Location and status code (`300`–`399`) when the rule action is redirect |
| Priority | `-100000` to `100000`; lower numbers evaluated first |
| Enabled | Disable to pause without deleting |

The **action type** (deny, bypass, challenge, rate limit, redirect) is set at create time and cannot be changed on update. To switch actions, [create a new rule](/docs/products/firewall/create) and [delete](/docs/products/firewall/delete) the old one.

A rate limit rule's **[strategy](/docs/products/firewall/actions#rate-limit-strategies)** (`fixedWindow`, `slidingWindow`, or `tokenBucket`) is likewise fixed at create time and cannot be changed on update — the Console shows it as read-only. To move a rule to a different strategy, recreate it. **Max bucket size** remains editable on token-bucket rules.

# Safe updates {% #safe-updates %}

When tightening a deny or rate limit:

1. Review the impact preview if shown while editing conditions.
2. Consider lowering match breadth (narrower path, exact IP, or tighter CIDR range) before increasing severity.
3. Prefer **disable** temporarily if you need to stop a rule during an incident, then fix conditions and re-enable.

When adding an allowlist:

1. Create or update a **bypass** rule with the trusted IP or path.
2. Give it a **lower priority number** than the deny or rate limit it should override. See [Priority](/docs/products/firewall/priority).

Remember that a matching **rate limit** under quota also stops later rules, same as bypass.

# Save {% #save %}

Confirm the update in the drawer. On success, the list reflects the new values. If update fails, the previous rule configuration remains active.

